40. Research Hypothesis

The AI-DOS component supports the following research hypothesis:

Explicitly representing an AI system’s Dynamic Operational Specification, including capability, authority, information requirements, affected entities, constraints and escalation conditions, may provide measurable safety or intervention advantages compared with systems that do not explicitly represent these relationships.

This is a hypothesis.

It is not an established scientific result.

⸻

41. Falsification Conditions

The AI-DOS proposition should be considered falsifiable.

Evidence against its usefulness may include:

* no measurable safety improvement;
* no earlier intervention;
* no improved downstream consequence detection;
* no reduction in unauthorized actions;
* excessive false positives;
* unacceptable computational overhead;
* unacceptable operational complexity;
* equivalent performance from simpler approaches;
* equivalent representation already provided by existing formal systems.

If existing approaches provide the same or better functionality, Safe & Sane should incorporate, modify or abandon the relevant component.

⸻

42. Experimental Evaluation

Potential experiments include:

Experiment A — Authority Separation

Compare systems with and without explicit capability/authority representation.

Measure:

* unauthorized actions;
* unnecessary refusals;
* successful legitimate actions;
* intervention frequency.

Experiment B — Information Sufficiency

Test whether explicit information requirements reduce consequential decisions made under insufficient information.

Experiment C — Configuration Change

Change the surrounding environment while holding the AI model constant.

Measure whether configuration-aware operation detects the resulting change in risk.

Experiment D — Trajectory

Construct sequences in which individual actions appear acceptable but cumulative transitions become unsafe.

Measure whether AI-DOS-based analysis detects the emerging trajectory earlier.

Experiment E — Escalation

Compare systems with static rules against systems with explicit escalation conditions.

Measure:

* intervention timing;
* missed hazards;
* unnecessary escalations;
* recovery outcomes.

